Abstract

We present a fully first-principles theoretical framework for emergent cognition in distributed multi-agent systems constrained by light-speed communication delays. Stellar-scale Dyson swarms serve as the physically grounded motivating context: each macro-agent operates an autonomous energy-harvesting swarm and communicates with neighbours only via channels whose latency is dictated by interstellar distance. Every quantity—from orbital dynamics and radiative transfer to information-theoretic synergy—is derived ab initio without reliance on external simulation artefacts.

The Delayed Predictive Consensus (DPC) architecture equips each agent with a temporal graph neural network incorporating explicit sinusoidal delay embeddings, graph attention, and a GRU-based recurrent state. Communication operates through a learned information bottleneck, enabling bandwidth-efficient latent-space messaging that degrades gracefully under quantisation and packet loss.

Cognitive Coherence is defined via Partial Effective Information Decomposition (PEID), a redundancy-free, interventionist information measure that yields closed-form Gaussian solutions via normalising flows and resolves the XOR inconsistency that plagues classical Partial Information Decomposition. PEID is axiomatically compatible with the major PID axioms, provides a unified toolkit for hyperedge causal graphs and cross-scale causal mechanisms, and admits higher-order multivariate extensions with provable O(1/√m) approximation error from stochastic synergy search.

Stability is established through a hierarchy of proofs. A Lyapunov–Krasovskii functional with individual-delay decomposition guarantees exponential consensus under heterogeneous, time-varying delays arising from stellar kinematics, incorporating both transmission and reaction delays via a Halanay-type inequality. For the full nonlinear GRU predictor, we apply neural vector Lyapunov–Razumikhin certificates that enable scalable input-to-state stability certification without requiring a common Lyapunov function—critical for network-size-independent bounds. The small-gain theorem for delayed neural fields, combined with neural operator universal approximation theory, proves semiglobal practical stability with computable contraction constant γ.

Lyapunov-Constrained Self-Modification (LCSM) enables safe architectural meta-learning through delay-robust primal-dual gradient dynamics. The dual variable evolution is augmented with an auxiliary state coupled through a gain matrix, with LMI-based tuning conditions that guarantee uniform asymptotic stability under bounded, time-varying delays while preserving the optimal solution. Convergence is guaranteed via distributed dual-descent even under heterogeneous communication lags.

The theoretical phase space is analytically mapped as a function of the dimensionless delay ratio ρ and coupling strength κ. Using graphon mean-field theory for the infinite-agent limit of delayed coupled oscillators—where bifurcations to synchrony in the mean-field model are proven to persist in large random networks—we derive the exact critical delay threshold ρ_c = (1 − λ_max)/(κ‖L_G‖_∞) and characterise bifurcations between stable cognition, oscillatory overshoot, fragmentation, and federation regimes. Multimodal graphons capturing stellar clustering give rise to the federation regime, where multiple internally coherent but externally loosely coupled clusters coexist, analogous to the "multicluster splitting" phenomena observed in mean-field coupled delay networks.

Two-timescale error bounds, derived via finite-time analysis of projected two-time-scale stochastic approximation with Polyak–Ruppert averaging, guarantee high-probability boundedness of the composite Lyapunov function. The mean-square error is decomposed into an approximation error (determined by the constrained subspace) and a statistical error (decaying sublinearly), with constants expressed through restricted stability margins and a coupling invertibility condition. Physics integration noise is tightened to O(Δt_phys⁴) through symplectic integrator variance properties, with martingale concentration yielding high-probability guarantees.

Robustness against cascading failures is quantified via the Average Value-at-Risk framework: closed-form expressions reveal explicit dependencies on the Laplacian spectrum, communication delay, and noise statistics, providing feasibility certificates for whether desired safety goals can be achieved without exhaustive topology search. The incremental risk assessment framework further enables scalable propagation of conditional risk as new failures are detected. Stability under Markovian packet loss is established through stochastic hybrid delayed system analysis.

Regret bounds of Õ(√(ND + d_h²)T) are obtained via information-directed sampling, linking the predictive horizon ℓ_max to rate-distortion theory and the communication bottleneck. The emergence threshold C > 0.6 is proved to imply measurable collective performance gains—increased swarm energy efficiency and reduced collision rates—with explicit constants derived from the Lipschitz constants of the swarm dynamics and the sensitivity of the control policy to prediction errors.

A complete conceptual architectural specification—the Galactic Cognition Simulator—is provided, comprising modular interfaces for physics integration, delayed message routing, DMPN inference, distributed meta-learning, and coherence estimation, sufficient for any future empirical implementation.

Keywords: Multi-Agent Systems, Communication Delays, Graph Neural Networks, Lyapunov–Krasovskii Stability, Emergent Cognition, Distributed Intelligence, Dyson Swarm, Partial Effective Information Decomposition, Small-Gain Theorem, Neural Operators, Graphon Theory, Information Bottleneck

Chapter 1 — Introduction

1.1 The Latency–Autonomy Paradox

Dyson swarms—vast constellations of energy-harvesting satellites orbiting a star—constitute a plausible engineering pathway toward Type II civilisation on the Kardashev scale. Recent theoretical treatments have demonstrated that autonomous collision avoidance, orbital stability, and energy collection can, in principle, be achieved around a single star through decentralised control. However, extending such coordination to multiple stellar systems introduces a constraint rooted in fundamental physics: the finite speed of light.

Interstellar communication delays range from years to centuries. A message sent from Proxima Centauri to the Sun requires approximately 4.25 years; across a galactic-scale civilisation, latencies can exceed 10⁵ years. This renders traditional synchronous multi-agent coordination impossible and creates what we term the latency–autonomy paradox:

How can a distributed system maintain coherent collective behaviour when no agent has access to a consistent global state, and all information from other agents is fundamentally obsolete?

This paradox compels a fundamental re-examination of distributed intelligence. If agents cannot share a consistent global state, can they nonetheless develop a shared understanding of their environment? Can they safely adapt their own computational architectures without destabilising the collective? Under what conditions does fragmentation become inevitable?

1.2 Scope and Methodology

This work is a purely mathematical and architectural contribution. It contains no numerical simulations or experimental data. Instead, it provides:

1. A complete first-principles mathematical formalism: every quantity—from the physical state of a single satellite to the global information-theoretic coherence metric—is derived from Newtonian mechanics, radiative transfer, thermodynamics, and Shannon information theory. No external simulation code or pre-existing dataset is assumed.
2. Rigorous stability proofs: a hierarchy of results spanning Lyapunov–Krasovskii functionals with individual-delay decomposition and Halanay-type inequalities, neural vector Lyapunov–Razumikhin certificates for scalable ISS, small-gain ISS for the nonlinear GRU predictor, neural operator universal approximation theorems, and delay-robust primal-dual convergence guarantees.
3. Analytical phase-space characterisation: the system's macroscopic behaviour is mapped as a function of two dimensionless control parameters—delay ratio ρ and coupling strength κ—derived from graphon mean-field limits of delayed coupled oscillators with support for multimodal graphons.
4. A complete architectural specification: the Galactic Cognition Simulator (GCS) blueprint, expressed as formal interfaces, data flow diagrams, and algorithmic pseudocode, sufficient for any future implementation.

1.3 The Three Fundamental Constraints

Current multi-agent systems research typically assumes conditions that are invalid in interstellar settings:

C1 — Asynchronous, massively delayed, heterogeneous communication. Most MARL algorithms assume negligible delays (τ < 10 timesteps). Here, delays are measured in tens to millions of decision timesteps, are heterogeneous across agent pairs due to differences in communication environments and processing speeds, and vary continuously with stellar motion.

C2 — Non-stationary environment and topology. Stellar kinematics continuously alter the communication graph and incident radiation field on millennial timescales. The graph itself is a dynamical object governed by orbital mechanics, requiring switched-system analysis with average dwell-time conditions.

C3 — Fully decentralised coordination. No central controller, shared reward signal, or global clock exists. Agents must coordinate using only locally observed, delayed, and possibly corrupted (quantised, packet-dropped) messages.

These constraints define the core research problem:

How can a network of autonomous agents, communicating only via time-delayed, heterogeneous, unreliable channels, with no central coordinator and a time-varying topology, develop coherent shared models of their environment, safely adapt their own computational architectures, and remain robust to cascading failures?

