3.2 Component Specifications

PhysicsEngine: Integrates satellite dynamics using symplectic Euler at Δt_phys substeps. Tracks gravitational, radiative, PR drag, multi-body, and stochastic impulsive accelerations. Computes raw 6-dim state vector including thermal risk metric. Maintains Lipschitz constant L_phys of orbital dynamics.

StateBuffer: Circular buffer of capacity τ_max + 1 storing normalised states. Push at each ML step; O(1) retrieval at arbitrary lag via index arithmetic.

LatentEncoder: Variational information bottleneck encoder compressing d_s = 6 to d_z latent dimension. Supports stochastic quantisation to b bits/dim. Provides learned default embedding for packet loss replacement. Training minimises I(s;z) − β I(z;ŝ).

DMPN: Four-stage forward pass—sinusoidal delay embedding, graph attention with LeakyReLU, GRU recurrent update, MLP state prediction.

MetaModule: Proposes architectural updates via meta-gradient. Maintains local Lagrangian multiplier λ_i and auxiliary state ξ_i. Participates in delay-robust PDGD consensus. Applies LCSM projection with allocated budget δ_i.

DelayRouter: Computes topology and delays from stellar positions at each ML step. Routes compressed messages with simulated delays. Handles packet loss (Markovian), quantisation, and topology switching.

MetaCoordinator: Logical bus facilitating dual variable exchange with simulated delays. Precomputes LMI gain matrix K. Allocates budgets δ_i from globally agreed ε.

MetricsCollector: Estimates pairwise and higher-order PEID synergy periodically. Detects phase region from ρ, κ, C. Computes cascading failure AVaR via Laplacian spectrum. Tracks regret. Updates incremental risk as new failures are detected.

3.3 Data Flow per ML Step

1. Physics integration: Each PhysicsEngine advances its swarm over Δt_ML with Δt_phys substeps, accumulating observables and tracking thermal risk.
2. Normalisation and buffering: Each agent normalises its raw state, pushes into circular buffer, encodes via latent encoder.
3. Message routing: Delay Router computes topology/delays, delivers {(z_j^{(t−τ_ji)}, τ_ji)} with simulated packet loss.
4. Prediction: DMPN forward pass: delay encoding, attention aggregation, GRU update, state prediction. Loss ℓ_i computed.
5. Coherence estimation (every T_pid): Random pair sample; GPID closed-form evaluated on latent Gaussian representations; higher-order synergy optionally computed on hypergraph clusters.
6. Meta-learning (every T_meta): Each agent proposes Δα_i; delay-robust PDGD exchanges dual variables via meta-coordinator; budgets δ_i allocated; LCSM projection applied.
7. Risk assessment: Laplacian spectrum of G⁽ᵗ⁾ and current delays fed into AVaR model; incremental risk update if new failures detected.

Chapter 4 — Rigorous Stability Analysis, Contraction Proofs, and Bounds

4.1 Individual-Delay-Reflected Lyapunov–Krasovskii Stability

Consider the linearised DMPN hidden-state dynamics with heterogeneous time-varying delays and switching topology:

\mathbf{h}_i^{(t)} = W_{\text{rec}}\,\mathbf{h}_i^{(t-1)} + \sum_{j \in \mathcal{N}_i^{(t)}} A_{ij}^{(t)}\,\mathbf{h}_j^{(t-\tau_{ji}(t))}

where τ_ji(t) = τ_ji^trans(t) + τ_react includes both transmission and reaction delays.

Theorem 4.1 (Individual-Delay-Reflected Exponential Stability). Construct the Lyapunov–Krasovskii functional by dividing the integral term into intervals containing heterogeneous delays and considering augmented vectors with delay states and integral states, with zero equality conditions added to reduce conservatism:

\Phi(t) = \mathbf{h}(t)^\top P\,\mathbf{h}(t) + \sum_{i=1}^N \sum_{j \in \mathcal{N}_i} \int_{t-\tau_{ji}(t)}^t \mathbf{h}_j(s)^\top Q_{ij}\,\mathbf{h}_j(s)\,ds

Applying the N-dependent generalised integral inequality and a Halanay-type inequality yields the sufficient stability condition:

\lambda_{\max}(W_{\text{rec}}) + \max_{i,t} \sum_{j \in \mathcal{N}_i^{(t)}} \|A_{ij}^{(t)}\| \cdot \sqrt{\tau_{ji}^{\text{trans}}(t) + \tau_{\text{react}} + 1} < 1

Under this condition, the system is globally exponentially stable with rate at least −½ ln(1−δ/λ_min(P)).

Corollary 4.1 (Switching Topology with Average Dwell-Time). If the communication graph switches among a finite family of topologies and the union graph over any interval of length T_switch is connected, then the common LKF approach (Theorem 4.1) guarantees stability provided the average dwell-time satisfies τ_dwell ≥ ln(μ)/|ln(γ)|, where μ is the LKF ratio between modes.

Corollary 4.2 (Stochastic Impulsive Effects). Under Poisson impulsive perturbations with rate λ_imp and bounded jump magnitude, the mean-square exponential stability condition becomes:

\lambda_{\max}(W_{\text{rec}}) + \max_{i,t} \sum_{j} \|A_{ij}^{(t)}\| \cdot \sqrt{\tau_{ji}(t) + 1} + \lambda_{\text{imp}} \cdot \mathbb{E}[\|\Delta\|^2] < 1

where the additional term captures the destabilising effect of stochastic impulses.

4.2 Neural Vector Lyapunov–Razumikhin Certificates for Scalable ISS

To obtain network-size-independent stability bounds, we employ neural vector Lyapunov–Razumikhin certificates for discrete-time delayed interconnected systems.

Definition 4.1 (Scalable ISS via Vector Lyapunov–Razumikhin Functions). A vector function V(e) = [V₁(e₁), ..., V_N(e_N)]^⊤ is a vector Lyapunov–Razumikhin function for the delayed interconnected system if there exist class K functions α₁, α₂, a nonnegative matrix Γ with spectral radius ρ(Γ) < 1, and a constant c > 1 such that for each subsystem i:

\alpha_1(\|e_i\|) \leq V_i(e_i) \leq \alpha_2(\|e_i\|)

and whenever V_i(e_i(t)) ≥ c · max_{k=1,...,N} max_{s∈[t−τ_max, t]} V_k(e_k(s)),

V_i(e_i(t+1)) \leq \max\left\{\Gamma_{i1}V_1(e_1(t)),\ldots,\Gamma_{iN}V_N(e_N(t)), \gamma_i(\|\boldsymbol{\nu}\|_\infty)\right\}

Theorem 4.2 (Scalable ISS via Neural Vector LRF). There exists a neural network parameterising V_i and Γ that can be synthesised and verified on reachability-constrained delay domains. The verification framework learns the neural certificates and proves the sISS property with scalability analysis, applicable to mixed-autonomy platoons, drone formations, and multi-agent networks with heterogeneous delays. The bound is network-size-independent: the verification complexity scales linearly with N rather than exponentially.

4.3 Small-Gain ISS for the Nonlinear GRU Predictor

Let h^★ be the ideal fixed point. Define error e⁽ᵗ⁾ = h⁽ᵗ⁾ − h^★. The GRU recurrence is nonlinear with Lipschitz activation functions (tanh, sigmoid, etc., each satisfying L_φ ≤ 1).

Theorem 4.3 (ISS via Small-Gain for Delayed GRU). Let γ_rec = λ_max(W_rec) L_φ and γ_delay = max_{i,t} Σ_j ∥A_ij⁽ᵗ⁾∥ L_ψ. If

\gamma_{\text{rec}} + \gamma_{\text{delay}} \cdot \max_i \tau_i < 1

then the error dynamics are ISS with linear asymptotic gain:

\|\mathbf{e}^{(t)}\| \leq M \lambda^t \|\mathbf{e}^{(0)}\| + \frac{\gamma_{\text{ISS}}}{1-\lambda} \|\boldsymbol{\nu}\|_\infty

for λ < 1 and γ_ISS = γ_delay / (1 − γ_rec). The proof employs the trajectory-based small-gain theorem for delayed neural fields, treating delayed terms as interconnections between subsystems.

4.4 Neural Operator Approximation and Semiglobal Practical Stability

The ideal predictor Ψ^★ maps the infinite history of delayed messages to the exact current state—this is the solution of a transport PDE (predictor feedback law).

Theorem 4.4 (Universal Approximation of Predictor Operators). For any ε > 0 and compact set Ω of initial states, there exists a neural operator Ψ_NN with finite parameters such that

\sup_{\mathbf{s} \in \Omega} \|\Psi^{\star}(\mathbf{s}) - \Psi_{\text{NN}}(\mathbf{s})\| \leq \varepsilon

The approximation holds for any black-box predictor satisfying the universal approximation error bound, including the GRU-based DMPN. Moreover, the predictor can be made delay-adaptive: the neural operator can approximate the predictor mapping for unknown delays, with Lyapunov–Krasovskii proof of semiglobal practical convergence to the true delay-compensated solution.

Theorem 4.5 (Semiglobal Practical Stability under Approximate Predictor). If the nominal conditions of Theorem 4.3 hold and the neural operator approximation error is bounded by ε_NN, then the closed-loop system achieves semiglobal practical stability:

\|\mathbf{e}^{(t)}\| \leq \beta(\|\mathbf{e}^{(0)}\|, t) + \mathcal{O}(\varepsilon_{\text{NN}})

The estimate is semiglobal: any desired ultimate bound B and initial set Ω can be achieved by choosing ε_NN sufficiently small, at the cost of increased neural operator complexity.

4.5 Complete Contraction Constant

Combining the above, the overall contraction factor of the DMPN closed-loop system is:

\gamma = \gamma_{\text{rec}} + \gamma_{\text{delay}} \cdot \tau_{\max} + L_{\alpha} \frac{\|\Delta\alpha\|_{\max}}{V}

The computable sufficient condition for γ < 1 is:

\lambda_{\max}(W_{\text{rec}}) + \kappa \cdot \|\mathcal{L}_G\|_\infty \cdot \tau_{\max} + \frac{\epsilon}{\mathcal{L}^{(t)}} + \lambda_{\text{imp}} \cdot \mathbb{E}[\|\Delta\|^2] < 1

For practical initialisation, W_rec is initialised via Glorot uniform such that λ_max(W_rec) ≤ 0.95; κ is controlled via attention dropout; and ε/L⁽ᵗ⁾ ≤ 10⁻³ by LCSM design. The impulsive term can be absorbed by imposing a maximum acceptable λ_imp.

4.6 Phase Diagram from Graphon Mean-Field Theory

Theorem 4.6 (Mean-Field Limit with Graphons). As N → ∞ with random geometric graph connectivity, the empirical distribution converges to the solution of the graphon mean-field equation. The stability of the incoherent state is determined by the graphon integral operator's spectral properties. Bifurcations to synchrony in the mean-field model persist in large random networks for large numbers of oscillators.

Theorem 4.7 (Critical Coupling and Phase Boundaries). Let W(x,y) be the limiting graphon and τ(x,y) the limiting delay kernel. Define:

\rho_c = \frac{1 - \lambda_{\max}}{\kappa \cdot \|\mathcal{L}_G\|_\infty}, \quad
\kappa_{\min} = \frac{1}{\Delta_{\max}}, \quad
\kappa_{\max} = 2\kappa_{\min}

The phase diagram comprises:

· Stable Cognition: ρ < ρ_c, κ ∈ [κ_min, κ_max] → C > 0.7, low prediction error.
· Oscillatory: ρ < ρ_c, κ > κ_max → 0.5 < C < 0.7, attention overshoot.
· Fragmentation: ρ > ρ_c → C < 0.5, agents behave as isolated learners.
· Federation (Multimodal Graphons): When W(x,y) is multimodal (multiple stellar clusters), and within-cluster delays are small while inter-cluster delays are large, multiple synchronised sub-clusters coexist. This corresponds to multicluster splitting phenomena in mean-field coupled delay networks, with C per cluster > 0.7 but inter-cluster C < 0.4.

4.7 Two-Timescale High-Probability Error Bound

Theorem 4.8 (Finite-Time Mean-Square Error Decomposition). Consider the two-timescale system with physics integration at Δt_phys and ML updates at Δt_ML = r Δt_phys. Let V_comp⁽ᵗ⁾ = L⁽ᵗ⁾ + γ_p ∥e_phys⁽ᵗ⁾∥². Then, for any δ > 0, with probability at least 1 − δ,

V_{\text{comp}}^{(t)} \leq \frac{\epsilon + C_1 r^{-2} + C_2 \Delta t_{\text{phys}}^4}{1-\gamma} + V_{\text{comp}}^{(0)} \gamma^t + C_3 \sqrt{\frac{\log(1/\delta)}{t}}

where C₁ captures the statistical averaging error (decaying sublinearly), C₂ captures the symplectic integrator's fourth-order energy variance, and C₃ captures martingale concentration. The mean-square error decomposes into approximation error (determined by the constrained subspace) and statistical error (controlled by the averaging horizon), with constants cleanly separated through restricted stability margins and a coupling invertibility condition.

4.8 Cascading Failure Risk Quantification

Theorem 4.9 (AVaR for Time-Delay Consensus Networks). For a time-delay consensus network with Laplacian \mathcal{L}_G, maximum delay τ_max, and noise variance σ², the Average Value-at-Risk at confidence level α satisfies the closed-form bound:

\text{AVaR}_\alpha \leq \sigma \sqrt{\frac{\lambda_{\max}(\mathcal{L}_G)}{2(1-\alpha)}} \cdot \frac{1}{1 - \tau_{\max} \cdot \lambda_{\max}(\mathcal{L}_G)}

This expression reveals the explicit roles of the Laplacian spectrum and communication delay in determining systemic risk. The bound serves as a feasibility certificate for safety goals; if the desired threshold exceeds this bound, no exhaustive topology search is required. Additionally, an efficient single-step update law enables scalable propagation of conditional risk as new failures are detected.

Theorem 4.10 (Stability under Markovian Packet Loss). Let the packet loss process for each edge be a Markov chain with stationary loss probability p̄. Under the DMPN contraction condition with γ < 1 when all messages arrive:

\gamma_{\text{loss}} = \gamma + (1-\gamma) L_{\text{default}} \cdot \bar{p}

Stability (γ_loss < 1) holds for p̄ < (1−γ)/((1−γ)L_default). For nonidentical Markov packet loss channels, the condition generalises to a weighted sum over heterogeneous loss probabilities, with the mean-square output consensus preserved under mild heterogeneity conditions.

4.9 Regret and Sample-Complexity Bounds

Theorem 4.11 (Bayesian Regret with Rate-Distortion Linkage). For N agents with maximum delay D = max τ_ji and bottleneck parameter β, the Bayesian regret after T ML steps using the DPC architecture with information-directed sampling satisfies

\text{Regret}(T) = \tilde{\mathcal{O}}\!\left(\sqrt{(N D + d_h^2)\,T}\right)

where the delay dependence is additive rather than multiplicative due to the predictor compensation. The information bottleneck β enters through the predictive horizon ℓ_max scaling:

\ell_{\max} \leq \tau_{\text{avg}} \cdot \frac{\lambda_{\min}(W_{\text{rec}})}{\lambda_{\max}(W_{\text{rec}})} \cdot \frac{1}{\beta}

Under quantised channels with b bits/dim, the optimal β is determined by solving the rate-distortion Lagrangian min_D [R(D) + βD] with R(D) = ½ log₂(σ_z²/D). As β increases, the effective horizon shrinks, requiring higher communication rates to maintain the same regret.

4.10 Emergence Threshold Implies Collective Gains

Theorem 4.12 (Coherence–Performance Link). Let C be the Cognitive Coherence. If C > 0.6, then relative to the isolated-agent baseline:

(i) η̄_swarm ≥ η_isolated · (1 + c₁(C − 0.6)) for c₁ = L_η · inf_{i,j} ∂η_i/∂ŝ_j.

(ii) R̄_coll ≤ R_isolated · (1 − c₂(C − 0.6)) for c₂ = L_R · inf_{i,j} ∂R_i/∂ŝ_j.

(iii) E_coh ≤ E_isolated − Δ_syn, where Δ_syn > 0 is proportional to the network-averaged synergy.

The constants c₁, c₂ are derived from the Lipschitz constants of the swarm dynamics and the sensitivity of the control policy to prediction errors. The proof uses control-theoretic coordination measures: synergistic information enables agents to anticipate neighbour states that would otherwise cause collisions or suboptimal energy collection.

