Appendix A — Lyapunov–Krasovskii Functional with Individual‑Delay Decomposition and Halanay Inequality

A.1 Construction of the Functional

Consider the linearised DMPN dynamics with heterogeneous time‑varying delays \tau_{ji}(t) = \tau_{ji}^{\text{trans}}(t) + \tau_{\text{react}} and switching topology G^{(t)}:

\mathbf{h}_i^{(t)} = W_{\text{rec}}\mathbf{h}_i^{(t-1)} + \sum_{j\in\mathcal{N}_i^{(t)}} A_{ij}^{(t)}\mathbf{h}_j^{(t-\tau_{ji}(t))}.

We stack the hidden states into a vector \mathbf{H}(t) = [\mathbf{h}_1(t)^\top,\dots,\mathbf{h}_N(t)^\top]^\top. Following Lee, Lee & Park (2026), define the augmented state vector

\tilde{\mathbf{H}}(t) = \begin{bmatrix}
\mathbf{H}(t) \\
\mathbf{H}(t-1) \\
\sum_{k=1}^{\tau_{\max}} \mathbf{H}(t-k)
\end{bmatrix}

and construct the Lyapunov–Krasovskii functional

\Phi(t) = \tilde{\mathbf{H}}(t)^\top P \tilde{\mathbf{H}}(t) + \sum_{i=1}^N\sum_{j\in\mathcal{N}_i} \int_{t-\tau_{ji}(t)}^{t} \mathbf{h}_j(s)^\top Q_{ij}\mathbf{h}_j(s)\,ds,

where P>0, Q_{ij}>0. The integral term is split into sub‑intervals each containing a specific heterogeneous delay; zero equality conditions (Lemma 1 of Lee et al.) are added to reduce conservatism.

A.2 Forward Difference and Halanay‑Type Inequality

The forward difference \Delta\Phi(t) = \Phi(t+1) - \Phi(t) is bounded using the generalised integral inequality (Lee et al., IFAC WC 2026). This yields

\Delta\Phi(t) \le -\delta\|\tilde{\mathbf{H}}(t)\|^2,

provided the matrix inequality

\lambda_{\max}(W_{\text{rec}}) + \max_{i,t}\sum_{j}\|A_{ij}^{(t)}\|\sqrt{\tau_{ji}(t)+1} < 1

holds. The Halanay‑type inequality (Continelli et al. 2026, Lemma 2) then gives exponential convergence:

\Phi(t) \le \Phi(0)\left(1 - \frac{\delta}{\lambda_{\min}(P)}\right)^t.

A.3 Switching Topology and Impulsive Effects

For switched topologies with average dwell‑time \tau_{\text{dwell}} satisfying \tau_{\text{dwell}} > \ln\mu/|\ln(1-\delta/\lambda_{\min}(P))| (where \mu is the maximal ratio of the LKF between modes), exponential stability is preserved. Stochastic impulses add the term \lambda_{\text{imp}}\mathbb{E}[\|\Delta\|^2] to the stability condition via the impulsive Halanay inequality (Lemma 3 of Continelli et al.), yielding Corollary 4.2.

