Appendix B — Neural Vector Lyapunov–Razumikhin Certificates for Scalable ISS

B.1 Lyapunov–Razumikhin Condition

For each subsystem i with error e_i, a candidate certificate is a neural network V_i(e_i;\theta_i) satisfying

\alpha_1(\|e_i\|) \le V_i(e_i) \le \alpha_2(\|e_i\|)

and, for all e_i such that V_i(e_i(t)) \ge c\max_{k,\;s\in[t-\tau_{\max},t]}V_k(e_k(s)),

V_i(e_i(t+1)) \le \max_{j}\{\Gamma_{ij}V_j(e_j(t)),\; \gamma_i(\|\nu\|_\infty)\}.

Here \Gamma = [\Gamma_{ij}] is a nonnegative matrix with spectral radius \rho(\Gamma)<1. This vector LRF directly captures the interconnection.

B.2 Synthesis via Reachability‑Constrained Learning

The neural certificates are learned by sampling trajectories over a delay‑domain reachable set. Verification of the Razumikhin inequality is done using SMT solvers over the neural network output; the framework of neural sISS certificates (2026) guarantees that the bound is network‑size‑independent because each V_i is verified independently, and the spectral radius condition \rho(\Gamma)<1 is a global check dependent only on the interconnection graph, not N. The complexity scales linearly with N.

B.3 Scalability Proof

By construction, the verification condition involves only local neighbours and the spectral radius of \Gamma. Since \Gamma_{ij} \neq 0 only if |i-j|\le 1 in the communication graph, and the maximum degree is bounded, \rho(\Gamma) can be certified via Gershgorin discs, yielding an N-independent bound on the achievable contraction.

