Appendix D — Neural Operator Universal Approximation and Delay‑Adaptive Predictor

D.1 Predictor PDE and Operator Form

The ideal predictor \Psi^\star is the solution map from the delay‑differential equation: given a history \{\mathbf{s}(t-\tau)\}_{\tau\ge 0}, output the current state \mathbf{s}(t). This can be expressed as a transport PDE; its solution operator is continuous.

D.2 Universal Approximation Theorem

By the universal approximation theorem for neural operators (Bhan et al., 2025), for any compact set \Omega of input histories and \varepsilon>0, there exists a neural operator \Psi_{\text{NN}} such that

\sup_{\mathbf{S}_\text{hist}\in\Omega} \|\Psi^\star(\mathbf{S}_\text{hist}) - \Psi_{\text{NN}}(\mathbf{S}_\text{hist})\| \le \varepsilon.

The proof proceeds by approximating the Green's function of the transport PDE with a finite sum of tensor‑product Fourier modes, which can be emulated by a neural network.

D.3 Delay‑Adaptive Extension

When the delay is unknown, a separate neural operator estimates \tau from the received timestamp, and the predictor is composed with a time‑warping layer. The delay‑adaptive predictor achieves semiglobal practical convergence: for any desired ultimate bound, there exists a sufficiently large network such that the closed‑loop error remains within that bound. The Lyapunov–Krasovskii stability analysis is extended with an additional error term from delay estimation; the composite Lyapunov function absorbs this error.

