Appendix E — Graphon Mean‑Field Limit and Bifurcation Analysis

E.1 Graphon Limit of Random Geometric Graphs

For N agents placed uniformly on a sphere, the random geometric graph converges almost surely to a graphon W(x,y) = \mathbf{1}_{\|x-y\|\le R}. The associated integral operator \mathcal{W}: L^2[0,1]\to L^2[0,1] is

(\mathcal{W}h)(x) = \int_0^1 W(x,y) h(y) \tau(x,y)\,dy.

E.2 Mean‑Field Dynamics and Synchronisation

The mean‑field hidden‑state dynamics become

\frac{d\mathbf{h}_x}{dt} = \mathcal{F}\!\left(\mathbf{h}_x,\; \int_0^1 W(x,y)\mathbf{h}_y(t-\tau(x,y))\,dy\right).

Linearising around the incoherent state (\mathbf{h}_x = 0) yields a characteristic equation whose roots determine stability. The critical coupling is given by \kappa_c = (1-\lambda_{\max}(W_{\text{rec}}))/\rho(\mathcal{W}), where \rho(\mathcal{W}) is the spectral radius of \mathcal{W}. For random geometric graphs, \rho(\mathcal{W}) \approx \bar{\tau}\cdot p, where p is edge probability.

E.3 Bifurcation Analysis and Multimodal Graphons

When W has K well‑separated modes (clusters), the mean‑field system can support K synchronised clusters with different phases. This “multicluster splitting” (Neural fields 2026) corresponds to the federation regime. The bifurcation from incoherence to synchrony is shown to be persistent for large N by Bramburger & Holzer (2025), using the fact that the graphon limit is a compact operator, hence spectral convergence holds.

