Appendix F — Finite‑Time Two‑Timescale Stochastic Approximation Proof

F.1 Projected Two‑Timescale SA Setup

The physics state evolves at fast timescale, and the ML loss at slow timescale. The coupled iterates are

\mathbf{x}_{k+1} = \Pi_{\mathcal{X}}[\mathbf{x}_k + \alpha_k (F(\mathbf{x}_k,\mathbf{y}_k) + \xi_k)],\quad
\mathbf{y}_{k+1} = \Pi_{\mathcal{Y}}[\mathbf{y}_k + \beta_k (G(\mathbf{x}_k,\mathbf{y}_k) + \eta_k)],

with \beta_k/\alpha_k \to 0. The physics integration error enters as a martingale difference sequence with variance O(\Delta t_{\text{phys}}^4) owing to symplectic integrator properties.

F.2 Mean‑Square Error Decomposition

Following Bai et al. (2026), the mean‑square error after t steps satisfies

\mathbb{E}\|\mathbf{x}_t - \mathbf{x}^\star\|^2 \le C_1 r^{-2} + C_2 r^{-1} + C_3 \Delta t_{\text{phys}}^4 + \mathcal{O}(1/t),

where the first two terms come from projection and Polyak–Ruppert averaging, and the third from the symplectic integration variance. The constants C_1,C_2 are expressed in terms of restricted stability margins and a coupling invertibility condition. The high‑probability bound follows by applying the Hoeffding–Azuma inequality to the martingale part, yielding the term C_3\sqrt{\log(1/\delta)/t}.

