Appendix H — Regret and Sample‑Complexity Bound with Information‑Directed Sampling

H.1 Information‑Directed Sampling in Delayed MARL

We adopt the Bayesian regret framework of Zhang et al. (2024). The Information Ratio is defined as

\Psi_t = \frac{(\text{expected regret})^2}{\text{information gain}}.

With delay D, the standard regret bound accumulates a factor of D. However, the DPC predictor reduces the effective decision‑time regret penalty from D\times T to ND additive bias because the predictor compensates for delay in the state estimate used for action selection. The information gain is bounded by d_h^2 per agent per step. Summing over N agents yields the regret bound

\text{Regret}(T) = \tilde{O}(\sqrt{(ND + d_h^2)T}).

H.2 Rate‑Distortion and Information Bottleneck Linkage

The predictive horizon \ell_{\max} is the maximum lag for which the prediction error stays below a threshold. Using the operator approximation theory (Appendix D), the error after \ell steps scales as \ell \cdot \varepsilon_{\text{NN}}, where \varepsilon_{\text{NN}} is proportional to the compression distortion D. The rate‑distortion function R(D) gives the minimum bits to achieve distortion D. The bottleneck parameter \beta determines the Lagrangian trade‑off: minimising I(s;z) - \beta I(z;\hat{s}) corresponds to solving \min_D [R(D) + \beta D]. The optimal \beta under quantised channels yields the predicted horizon scaling \ell_{\max} \propto 1/\beta.

