Appendix I — PEID Gaussian Closed‑Form and Hypergraph Extension

I.1 Gaussian PID Formulas

Under a linear Gaussian model, the mutual information is

I(X;T) = \frac{1}{2}\log\frac{|\Sigma_{TT}|}{|\Sigma_{TT} - \Sigma_{TX}\Sigma_{XX}^{-1}\Sigma_{XT}|}.

The PEID synergy between X,Y and target T under maximum‑entropy interventions is

\text{Syn}(X,Y\to T) = I(X,Y;T)_{\text{int}} - I(X;T)_{\text{int}} - I(Y;T)_{\text{int}},

where the subscript _{\text{int}} indicates the effective information computed from the intervened joint distribution. Because the intervention sets \Sigma_{XX},\Sigma_{YY} to identity (after normalisation), the formulas simplify. The closed‑form solution can be computed in O(d^3) via Cholesky decomposition.

I.2 Hypergraph Extension

For K\ge 3 sources, PEID decomposes the joint effective information into terms for each subset:

EI(\{X_1,\dots,X_K\}\to T) = \sum_{k=1}^K EI(X_k\to T) + \sum_{\mathcal{S}\subseteq[K],|\mathcal{S}|\ge 2} \text{Syn}(\mathcal{S}\to T).

The synergetic atoms are computed by Möbius inversion over the lattice of subsets. The approximation error from stochastic synergy search with m random samples of subsets decays as O(1/\sqrt{m}) because the variance of the synergy estimator is bounded by the total mutual information, which is finite. Importance sampling guided by lower‑order synergies further reduces the constant.

