Appendix J — Distributed Delay‑Robust Primal‑Dual Gradient Dynamics Proof

J.1 Continuous‑Time Primal‑Dual Dynamics

The optimisation problem

\min_{\delta}\sum_i \ell_i(\delta_i)\quad\text{s.t.}\quad \sum_i \delta_i \le \epsilon,\;\; \delta_i\ge 0

is solved by the primal‑dual gradient flow

\dot{\delta}_i = -\nabla\ell_i(\delta_i) - \lambda_i,\quad
\dot{\lambda}_i = \sum_{j\in\mathcal{N}_i}(\lambda_j(t-\tau_{ji}) - \lambda_i(t)) + K\xi_i.

Here \xi_i is an auxiliary state. The gain matrix K is designed via LMI to guarantee that the delayed consensus term converges to zero and the Lagrangian multiplier reaches optimality.

J.2 LMI Conditions and Uniform Asymptotic Stability

Let \tilde{\lambda}_i = \lambda_i - \lambda^\star. The error dynamics are

\dot{\tilde{\lambda}} = -\alpha\mathcal{L}_G\tilde{\lambda} + \alpha\sum_{k=1}^{\tau_{\max}} B_k\tilde{\lambda}(t-k) + K\xi.

By choosing a Lyapunov–Krasovskii functional similar to Appendix A, one obtains LMI conditions on K and the delays such that the origin is uniformly asymptotically stable. The delay‑robust PDGD result (Mudrik et al., 2026) provides these conditions explicitly; they involve the maximum delay, graph connectivity, and gain margin.

J.3 Convergence to Optimal Allocation

With the LMI satisfied, \tilde{\lambda}(t)\to 0, so all \lambda_i converge to \lambda^\star. Then the primal dynamics converge to the solution of the distributed dual problem, giving \delta_i = \delta_i^\star. The projection step in LCSM ensures feasibility at each discrete meta‑update.

