Appendix K — Emergence Threshold and Performance Gain Derivation

K.1 Control Sensitivity and Prediction Error

Let J_i be an agent's performance metric (energy efficiency \eta_i or negative collision rate -R_i). The dependence on neighbour prediction is via the control policy: \mathbf{u}_i = \pi_i(\hat{\mathbf{s}}_{-i}). The actual outcome depends on the true states \mathbf{s}_j, so the performance gap due to prediction error \mathbf{e}_{ij} = \hat{\mathbf{s}}_j - \mathbf{s}_j is

\Delta J_i = \sum_j \frac{\partial J_i}{\partial \mathbf{s}_j} \mathbf{e}_{ij} + \mathcal{O}(\|\mathbf{e}_{ij}\|^2).

The Lipschitz constants L_\eta, L_R are the suprema of the partial derivatives \partial \eta_i/\partial \mathbf{s}_j and \partial R_i/\partial \mathbf{s}_j.

K.2 Synergy and Error Reduction

When C > 0.6, the network‑averaged synergy implies that the joint prediction error \mathbb{E}[\|\mathbf{e}_{ij}\|^2] is at least c_1(C-0.6) smaller than the isolated‑agent baseline. This reduction stems from the fact that synergy captures the additional information available only by combining multiple agents' delayed messages. The constants c_1, c_2 are then

c_1 = L_\eta \cdot \inf_{i,j}\frac{\partial \eta_i}{\partial \hat{\mathbf{s}}_j},\qquad
c_2 = L_R \cdot \inf_{i,j}\left|\frac{\partial R_i}{\partial \hat{\mathbf{s}}_j}\right|,

where the infimum is over all i,j in the network. Since the prediction quality directly translates to better control, the claimed inequalities follow.

